  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Wednesday...\n\nCurrent upper level pattern shows a transition to westerly flow\nwith an upper level low off the coast of the Pacific northwest\ncoast. Ensembles show this low moving in as shortwave troughing\ntoday, tightening the surface pressure gradient along the higher\nterrain of south central MT. This, along with 700 mb winds of \n35-50 kts will create a favorable pattern for strong gap winds for\nareas of Livingston through Big Timber and Harlowton. Timing for \nthe Livingston/Nye areas looks to be mid day today through \nThursday evening. For the remainder of the foothills area, strong \nwinds are expected to increase through the morning Friday, \ndecreasing into the evening. Use caution if traveling along I-90 \nfrom Big Timber to Livingston or Highway 191 from Big Timber to \nJudith Gap late Thursday into Friday. Consider alternate routes, \nespecially if driving a high profile vehicle. \n\nLooking at precipitation with this event, mountain snow is\nexpected to begin Thursday night with snow across the foothills \nbeginning late Friday. Much of the precipitaton should taper off \nlate Saturday. The Absaroka/Beartooths are currently forecasted to\nget 8-16 inches of snow. West to southwest slopes will be favored\nthrough Friday night, transitioning to northerly as 700 mb winds \nturn from the north. This will likely favor an upslope pattern \nfor the associated foothills. The Red Lodge foothills currently \nhave about a 50-70% chance of at least 4 inches of snow. The \nremainder of the area mountains (Crazies and Bighorns) are \ncurrently forecast to get around 6-9 inches of snow. With snow \nlevels only getting down to about 4,000 ft, much of the precip \ntype across the lower elevations is expected to be rain or a \nrain/snow mix. \n\nEnsembles hint at bringing in more unsettled weather at the\nbeginning of next week, but most of the energy seems to move\nsouth. This will be something to watch as it approaches.\n\nTemperatures are expected to remain in the 40s to low 50s through\nFriday, cooling to the 30s and 40s into next week. TS",
